    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Write each division using a fraction bar, without simplifying. For (a), also name the numerator and denominator.\n(a) $7\\div11$\n(b) $19\\div8$\n(c) $6\\div6$",
        "solution_text": "The first number goes on top and the number we divide by goes underneath.\n(a) $7\\div11=\\dfrac{7}{11}$\nThe numerator is $7$ and the denominator is $11$.\n(b) $19\\div8=\\dfrac{19}{8}$\n(c) $6\\div6=\\dfrac{6}{6}$\nKeep $\\dfrac{6}{6}$ because the question says not to simplify."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) Jo writes $23\\div6=\\dfrac{6}{23}$. Explain the error and write the correct fraction.\n(b) Complete $26\\div\\square=\\dfrac{26}{9}$.",
        "solution_text": "(a) Jo has reversed the two numbers.\nThe number being divided, $23$, goes on top.\n$23\\div6=\\dfrac{23}{6}$\n(b) The number we divide by goes underneath.\n$26\\div9=\\dfrac{26}{9}$\nThe missing number is $9$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A $7$-metre cord is shared equally among $10$ people. Record each share as a fraction of a metre.\n(b) $17$ litres of water are poured equally into $6$ jugs. Record the amount in each jug as a fraction of a litre. Do not change it to a mixed number.",
        "solution_text": "Divide the amount being shared by the number of equal shares.\n(a) $7\\div10=\\dfrac{7}{10}$ metre\n(b) $17\\div6=\\dfrac{17}{6}$ litres\nThe amount goes on top; the number of people or jugs goes underneath."
      }
    ],
